The bug in this code is that it only processes one character with the maximum frequency and then sets its count to zero, but it doesn't continue processing the rest of the characters. The algorithm should find all characters sorted by their frequencies in descending order.

In the current implementation:
1. It finds the character with maximum frequency (only once)
2. Appends that character to result string max times
3. Sets that character's count to 0
4. Then returns immediately without processing remaining characters

The fix requires a loop to process all characters, not just one.

## Corrected Code
```java
class Solution {
    public String frequencySort(String s) {
        char[] arr = new char[256];
    for(int i=0; i<s.length(); i++){
        arr[s.charAt(i)]++;    
    }

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

    while(true){
        int max = -1;
        char maxi = 0;
        for(char i=0; i<arr.length; i++){
            if(arr[i] > max){
                max = arr[i];
                maxi = i;
            }
        }
        if(max == 0) break;

        for(int i=0; i<max; i++){
            sb.append(maxi);   
        }
        arr[maxi] = 0;
    }

    return sb.toString();

    }
}
